        {
            "post_id": "day_20",
            "timestamp": "20260720_193310",
            "image": "images/day20_20260720_193310.jpg",
            "captions": {
                "linkedin": "Did you know a two‑year‑old can already be exposed to the same algorithmic traps that keep adults scrolling? I never imagined worrying about Instagram before my daughter could say “mama,” but today TikTok feels like it’s just around the corner of our living room.  \n\nHere are the red flags I’ve learned to watch for, and simple steps that have helped our family:  \n\n1️⃣ Screen time as a pacifier. Before she even taps an app, I make it a habit to sit together and explore content. Saying “let’s watch this together” builds a boundary that lasts longer than any device.  \n\n2️⃣ Stranger danger online. Direct messages, comments, or shared links can come from anyone. I’ve taught her that a screen can hide a stranger, and we practice never replying without a parent’s okay.  \n\n3️⃣ Early comparison. Even toddlers absorb messages about looks and behavior from short videos. A recent survey showed 89 % of parents feel kids spend too much time on screens, yet only half set limits. That gap tells us how quietly social media slips in.  \n\n4️⃣ The attention trap. Reels and TikTok are engineered to keep scrolling. I watch for restlessness when the device is taken away, difficulty focusing during play, or mood swings tied to screen access.  \n\n5️⃣ Content slipping past filters. Hashtags and challenges evolve faster than parental controls. I ask her every evening, “What did you watch today?” and really listen to her answer.  \n\nI’m not trying to create a tech‑free bubble-my phone is both a distraction and a lifeline during tantrums. What matters is open, casual conversation, not a lecture. By keeping the dialogue open, our kids learn to walk through the online world with us, not hide behind it.\n\n#DigitalGuardian #DigitalParenting #DigitalSafety #ParentingTips",
                "bluesky": "Before your toddler gets a TikTok account, set the rules: limit screen time, teach stranger‑danger 2.0, guard against comparison, watch the dopamine‑driven scroll trap, and audit content. Talk, don’t lecture, and keep the door open.\n\nWant to read more?... check out my LinkedIn"
            },
            "platforms_posted": []
        }
